  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 1249 PM MDT Fri Oct 2 2026\n\nDry and significantly warmer than average conditions expected\nfor the entirety of the long term period. The work week will\nstart with very warm conditions as a ridge of high pressure \ncontrols the Western US, with highs in the widespread upper 70's\nwest of the Laramie Range, and mid to upper 80's to nearing 90 \nto the east. Probabilistic guidance is in unanimous agreement on\nthis heat, with only a 3 degree spread noted in highs for the \nCheyenne area specifically. These highs will also be almost 20 \ndegrees above normal for this time of year, and record \ntemperatures will be on the line for several locations across \nthe forecast area on both sides of the mountains. Finally, this \nwill be the driest day of the week with RH values bottoming out \nin the mid to low teens. This alongside breezy daytime winds \ncould return us to critical fire weather conditions, and will \nneed to be monitored over the weekend for any necessary \nproducts.\n\nThe ridge will flatten out and shift a bit more southward \nthrough the rest of the week which will help slightly alleviate \nthese temperatures, but expect highs remaining in the 70's and \n80's through Friday. A slight increase in moisture could also be\npossible, but slight is the keyword here, and we will still see\nelevated fire weather danger thanks to RH values still dropping\ninto the upper teens to low 20% range. Our only indication of \nany hint of moisture occurs on Tuesday during this slight \npattern shift, with a single time period of a slight shower \nchance at the top of the mountains, so dry it's impressive. \nUnfortunately models are also in unanimous agreement on this \npattern, and the only variances noted in ensemble clusters is \nhow far east or west the ridge over our area shifts by the end \nof the forecast period. While this could slightly increase or \ndecrease our highs by the end of the week, the message remains \nthe same no matter what - hot and dry through the first full \nweek of October.",
